      "directNodeIds": [
        "math:real-exponent-laws",
        "cnm_h042"
      ],
      "explanation": "The bases \\(100\\) and \\(1000\\) are powers of \\(10\\), so using a common base lets us compare the exponents. For real \\(x\\), the positive-base power laws give\n\\[\n10^x\\cdot100^{2x}\n=10^x\\cdot(10^2)^{2x}\n=10^{x+4x}=10^{5x}.\n\\]\nAlso, \\(1000^5=(10^3)^5=10^{15}\\). Thus the equation becomes \\(10^{5x}=10^{15}\\).\n\nSince powers of \\(10\\) increase strictly as their real exponents increase, equal powers must have equal exponents. Therefore \\(5x=15\\), so \\(\\boxed{x=3}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
      "id": "rewrite-base-ten",
      "sourceRef": "amc/2016_AMC_10A_Q02#rewrite-base-ten",
      "title": "Express every power with base 10"
